The '''Marañón oil fires''' ({{lang-bl|Focs de Petroleu del Marañón}}) were a series of [[W:Oil well|oil well]] fires caused by [[Potocsí|Potocsino]] military forces during the final stages of the [[Camboriú War]] in late 1976 and early 1977. The fires resulted from a [[W:Scorched earth|scorched earth]] policy implemented by retreating Potocsino troops, who ignited approximately 612 oil wells, storage tanks, and processing facilities across the [[Marañón basin]] in an attempt to delay the Baliscan advance and deny Baliscan forces access to critical petroleum infrastructure.

The fires burned for approximately four and a half months, from 23 November 1976 until the final well was extinguished on 15 April 1977. They released massive quantities of [[W:Petroleum|crude oil]], [[W:Smoke|smoke]], and [[W:Air pollution|atmospheric pollutants]] into the surrounding environment, causing what is considered one of the worst environmental disasters in Abyalan history. The resulting [[W:Petroleum rain|petroleum rain]] and [[W:Smoke plume|smoke plumes]] contaminated vast areas of the [[Marañón rainforest]], the world's largest [[W:Temperate rainforest|temperate rainforest]], and caused severe health impacts for indigenous populations in the region.

The extinguishing operation, involving the [[Confederal Armed Forces]], the [[Baliscan Oil Corporation]] (COBAL), and international firefighting teams from seven countries, was one of the most complex and dangerous oil well firefighting operations ever undertaken. The fires became a defining symbol of the Camboriú War and remain a subject of environmental, medical, and historical research.

== Background ==
{{Main|Camboriú War|Marañón basin}}

=== The Marañón basin ===
The [[Marañón basin]] is a vast geographic region covering more than 8,000,000 km² (3,100,000 sq mi) in center of the Abyalan continent, with the majority located within the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]] (primarily within the communities of [[Pindorama]] and [[Bayara]]). The basin is bounded to the north and east by the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] mountain range, to the south by the Galibi Highlands, and to the west by the [[Salsipuedes Sea]].

The basin contains the [[Marañón rainforest]] (also known as Maranyon), the largest [[W:Temperate rainforest|temperate rainforest]] in the world, covering approximately 6,000,000 km² (2,300,000 sq mi). The Marañón rainforest is characterized by [[W:Conifer|coniferous]] and [[W:Broadleaf|broadleaf]] forests with high rainfall, moderate temperatures, and dense [[W:Undergrowth|undergrowth]]. The region is home to endemic species found nowhere else on Adonia, including the Marañón [[W:Redwood|redwood]] (''Sequoia maranyonis''), the giant [[W:Conifer|conifers]] that dominate the forest canopy, and the [[W:Araucaria araucana|piñonero]] (''Araucaria araucana'').

=== Oil discovery and development ===
Petroleum was first discovered in commercial quantities in the Marañón basin in 1938, near the settlement of [[Alcorcón]]. The discovery transformed the region into one of Balisca's most important petroleum-producing areas. By 1968, the Marañón basin accounted for approximately 75% of domestic oil production, with over 800 active wells operated primarily by the [[Essential industry|state-owned]] [[Baliscan Oil Corporation]] (COBAL) and several smaller enterprises.

The oil fields were concentrated in three main areas:

* The '''Albacete field''' — the oldest and largest field, with approximately 340 wells
* The '''Yurimaguas field''' — a secondary field with approximately 210 wells
* The '''Iquitos field''' — a newer development with approximately 180 wells

The [[Marañón Delta]], formed by the river's deposition of alluvial soils, contained extensive storage and processing facilities, including refineries at [[Iquitos]] and [[Yurimaguas]], and export terminals along the [[Salsipuedes Sea]].

=== The Camboriú War ===
{{Main|Camboriú War}}
The [[Camboriú War]] ({{lang-bl|Guerra de Camboriú}}) was a three-month armed conflict between the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]] and the [[Republic of Potocsí]] that took place from October 1977 to January 1977. The war was part of the larger [[Balisca-Potocsí conflict]], related to disputes over Baliscan-controlled territories in the Marañón basin.

The conflict followed years of escalating tensions, including troop mobilizations, border violations, and airspace incursions by both parties. Potocsí claimed sovereignty over portions of the Marañón basin, particularly the oil-rich [[Añãngaraçú]] region, arguing that the territory had been unjustly seized during the [[Marañón War]] (1921–1923).

On 26 October 1976, Potocsino forces launched a surprise invasion of the Marañón basin, rapidly advancing through the western portions of the region. Initial Potocsino successes included the capture of several oil fields and the city of [[Yurimaguas]]. However, Baliscan forces, under the command of General [[Elena Varela]], mounted a determined defense and eventually reversed the Potocsino advance.

By November 1976, Baliscan forces had begun a counteroffensive that pushed Potocsino troops back toward the border. As Potocsino forces retreated, they implemented a scorched earth policy, destroying infrastructure and setting fire to oil wells across the basin.

== The fires ==

=== Ignition ===
The first oil fires were reported on 23 November 1976, as Potocsino forces began their retreat from the Albacete field. According to Baliscan military intelligence, the order to ignite the wells came directly from Potocsino General [[Ramón Velázquez]], who had been instructed to "deny the enemy the resources to continue their war effort."

Potocsino combat engineers used [[W:Explosive charges|explosive charges]] to rupture wellheads and ignite the escaping crude oil. In some cases, they pumped crude oil into storage tanks and set them ablaze before detonating the tanks to spread the fires. The operation was systematic: Potocsino forces targeted the most productive wells first, working through the fields methodically as they withdrew.

By 1 December 1976, approximately 612 oil wells were burning across the Marañón basin:
