=== Scale and intensity ===
The burning wells produced massive [[Fire|flames]] reaching heights of 30–60 meters (100–200 feet), with some particularly high-pressure wells producing flames exceeding 100 meters (330 feet). The heat was intense enough to melt steel equipment and prevent approach within 50 meters of many wells.

The fires consumed an estimated 5–6 million [[W:Barrel (unit)|barrels]] (790,000–950,000 m³) of oil per day at their peak, representing approximately 40% of Balisca's total pre-war production capacity. The economic loss was estimated at S/4.3 billion (1976 values).

=== Smoke plumes ===
The fires produced massive [[Smoke plume|smoke plumes]] that rose to altitudes of 3–5 kilometers (10,000–16,000 feet) and spread over vast areas. The [[smoke]] contained [[W:Soot|soot]], [[W:Sulfur dioxide|sulfur dioxide]], [[W:Nitrogen oxides|nitrogen oxides]], [[W:Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon|polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons]] (PAHs), and other [[toxic]] compounds.

Satellite imagery from December 1976 showed smoke plumes extending more than 1,500 kilometers (930 miles) from the source, covering an area of approximately 2.5 million square kilometers (965,000 sq mi). The plumes reached the [[Galibi Highlands]] to the south, the [[Matarrañya]] mountains to the east, and extended over the [[Salsipuedes Sea]] to the west.

The smoke reduced [[W:Sunlight|solar radiation]] at the surface by 60–90% in affected areas, causing [[W:Daytime|daytime]] temperatures to drop by 5–10 °C (9–18 °F). In some areas, the sun was not visible for weeks at a time, creating an effect described by survivors as "eternal twilight."

=== Petroleum rain ===
As smoke particles cooled and condensed in the atmosphere, they formed droplets of [[Petroleum|oil]] and [[soot]] that fell as "[[W:Black rain|black rain]]" or "[[petroleum rain]]." This phenomenon was reported across the Marañón basin and adjacent regions, contaminating [[water]] supplies, [[soil]], and [[vegetation]].

The petroleum rain was particularly damaging to the [[Marañón rainforest]], where the [[temperate rainforest]] ecosystem was coated in oily residues. The [[Conifer|coniferous]] trees that dominated the forest canopy were especially vulnerable, as the oil coated their needles and prevented [[photosynthesis]].

== Environmental impact ==

=== Immediate effects ===
The immediate environmental effects of the fires were catastrophic:

* '''Air quality:''' [[Air pollution]] levels exceeded safe limits by factors of 100–1,000 in affected areas. [[Particulate matter]] (PM10) concentrations reached 10,000–20,000 μg/m³, compared to safe limits of 50–150 μg/m³.
* '''Water contamination:''' [[Petroleum]] and [[toxic]] compounds contaminated [[River|rivers]], [[Stream|streams]], and [[groundwater]] across the basin. The [[Marañón River]] and its tributaries carried oil slicks for hundreds of kilometers.
* '''Soil contamination:''' [[Oil spill|Oil spills]] and petroleum rain contaminated [[soil]] across an estimated 250,000 km² (96,500 sq mi) of the Marañón basin.
* '''Vegetation damage:''' The [[Marañón rainforest]] suffered extensive damage, with an estimated 15,000 km² (5,800 sq mi) of forest severely affected by fire, heat, or contamination.

=== Impact on the Marañón rainforest ===
The [[Marañón rainforest]], the world's largest [[temperate rainforest]], was particularly affected by the fires. Unlike tropical rainforests, temperate rainforests are adapted to cooler, wetter conditions and are less resilient to fire and contamination.

The impact on the forest included:

* '''Canopy damage:''' The [[Conifer|coniferous]] trees that form the forest canopy—particularly the Marañón [[redwood]] (''Sequoia maranyonis''), Marañón [[spruce]] (''Picea maranyonis''), and Marañón [[fir]] (''Abies maranyonis'')—suffered extensive needle loss due to petroleum coating and heat stress.
* '''Understory mortality:''' The dense [[undergrowth]] of [[Fern|ferns]], [[Moss|mosses]], and [[Shrub|shrubs]] was killed over large areas by oil contamination and lack of sunlight.
* '''Wildlife impacts:''' [[Bird|Birds]], [[Mammal|mammals]], and [[Amphibian|amphibians]] suffered population declines due to habitat loss, contamination, and respiratory effects from smoke inhalation.
* '''Soil ecosystem disruption:''' The [[soil]] ecosystem, including [[fungi]], [[bacteria]], and [[Invertebrate|invertebrates]], was disrupted by petroleum contamination.

An estimated 3–5% of the Marañón rainforest was severely damaged by the fires and their aftermath, with an additional 10–15% affected by smoke, petroleum rain, and secondary contamination.

=== Long-term environmental effects ===
The long-term environmental effects of the fires continue to be studied:

* '''Persistent contamination:''' [[Petroleum]] and [[PAH|PAHs]] persist in [[soil]] and [[sediment]] in some areas, particularly in wetland and riparian zones.
* '''Forest recovery:''' The [[Marañón rainforest]] has shown remarkable resilience, with most severely affected areas showing significant recovery after 50 years. However, some areas remain contaminated, and full ecosystem recovery may take centuries.
* '''Water quality:''' [[Groundwater]] contamination persists in some areas, though [[river]] water quality has largely recovered.
* '''Wildlife populations:''' Most [[wildlife]] populations have recovered, though some species—particularly [[Amphibian|amphibians]] sensitive to water quality—remain below pre-war levels.

== Health effects ==

=== Immediate health impacts ===
The fires caused severe health impacts for civilian populations in the Marañón basin:

* '''Respiratory effects:''' [[Smoke]] inhalation caused [[asthma]], [[bronchitis]], and other respiratory conditions in an estimated 200,000+ people. Hospitals reported a 500% increase in respiratory admissions during the fires.
* '''Cardiovascular effects:''' Exposure to [[particulate matter]] and [[air pollution]] increased the risk of [[heart attack]] and [[stroke]] in vulnerable populations.
* '''Dermatological effects:''' [[Petroleum]] and [[soot]] exposure caused [[skin irritation]], [[Rash|rashes]], and [[Chemical burn|chemical burns]].
* '''Ophthalmological effects:''' [[Smoke]] and [[Irritant|irritants]] caused [[eye]] irritation, [[conjunctivitis]], and temporary [[blindness]] in some cases.

At least 12 firefighters and 8 oil workers died during the extinguishing operation, primarily due to [[Explosion|explosions]], [[Fire|burns]], and [[Accident|accidents]]. An additional 234+ people suffered injuries requiring hospitalization.

=== Long-term health effects ===
Long-term health effects continue to be studied:

* '''Cancer rates:''' Some studies have suggested elevated rates of [[W:Lung cancer|lung cancer]] and other [[W:Cancer|cancers]] in populations heavily exposed to the fires, though results are inconclusive.
* '''Chronic respiratory disease:''' Rates of [[W:Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease|chronic obstructive pulmonary disease]] (COPD) and [[asthma]] remain elevated in some heavily affected communities.
* '''Mental health:''' The [[W:Psychological trauma|psychological trauma]] of the fires—displacement, loss of livelihood, and environmental destruction—has had lasting effects on mental health in affected communities.

== Extinguishing operation ==

=== Organization ===
The operation to extinguish the fires was organized by the [[Confederal Emergency Management Agency (Balisca)|Confederal Emergency Management Agency]] (AGEC) in coordination with the [[Confederal Armed Forces]] and the [[Baliscan Oil Corporation]] (COBAL). Given the scale of the disaster, Balisca requested international assistance, and firefighting teams from seven countries participated in the operation.

The operation was commanded by General [[Elena Varela]], who had led the Baliscan counteroffensive during the war, and [[Jordi Puig]], the chief engineer of COBAL.

