== Aftermath ==

=== Economic impact ===
The fires had severe economic consequences for Balisca:

* '''Oil production:''' The Marañón basin's oil production capacity was reduced by approximately 80% immediately after the fires. Full production has not recovered, .
* '''Infrastructure damage:''' The destruction of wells, storage tanks, refineries, and export facilities caused an estimated S/7.5 billion in damage (1976 values).
* '''Environmental remediation:''' Cleanup and remediation costs were estimated at ₡1.2 billion over the following decade.
* '''Lost revenue:''' The Baliscan government estimated lost oil revenue at S/13.8 billion during the period of reduced production.

=== Political consequences ===
{{Main|Camboriú War#Aftermath}}
The fires reinforced the Baliscan government's hardline stance against Potocsí and contributed to the diplomatic reorientation of the country:

* The government of Trotskyist–Posadist [[Mário Sá Justino]] was bolstered by the successful performance of the Armed Forces and their hardline stance against diplomatic negotiation with Potocsí.
* Relations with [[Breisland]] and [[Volisania]] deteriorated, as both countries were perceived as insufficiently supportive of Balisca during the conflict.
* Balisca strengthened ties with [[Burawa]] and the [[Socialist Bloc]], seeking allies against perceived Western hostility.
* The [[Confederal Armed Forces]] underwent a substantial buildup, with defense spending increasing from 3.2% to 5.8% of GDP by 1972.

=== Diplomatic resolution ===
{{Main|Kuluba Accords}}
Diplomatic relations between Balisca and Potocsí were restored in 1984 following years of negotiation fostered by the governments of [[Asikyira]] and [[Galibi]]. The two governments issued a joint statement on normalization, though no change in either country's position regarding the status of the Marañón basin was made explicit until the signing of the [[1982 Kuluba Accords]].

The Kuluba Accords established a framework for cooperation on oil production and environmental remediation in the disputed region, though sovereignty disputes remain unresolved.

